{#
  Post-quantum SSH key exchange.

  /native/ip/ssh/server/algorithm/kex/kex-options        (ordered-by user)
  /native/ip/ssh/server/algorithm/hostkey/hostkey-options (ordered-by user)

  Note there is no separate namespace here. Cisco-IOS-XE-ip is a SUBMODULE of
  Cisco-IOS-XE-native, not a module that augments it, so these nodes are in the native
  namespace and the payload carries one xmlns.

  Both leaf-lists are ordered-by user, which means the order in this document is the
  offer order and the first entry is what a capable client lands on. So the whole
  container is pushed with nc:operation="replace" rather than merged: a merge would add
  entries in whatever position the box felt like, and "which algorithm did we actually
  negotiate" would become a coin flip.

  26.2 offers three hybrids and no pure ML-KEM, which is the right call: a break in
  either half still leaves you the other. The pure-PQ option does not exist, so you
  cannot accidentally pick it.

  The hostkey list is not a PQ setting. It is the thing that stops importing an EC
  keypair from locking you out of the box: the server starts advertising
  ecdsa-sha2-nistp256 and then fails every handshake that selects it. Pin it before you
  import anything.
-#}
{% set op = 'remove' if state == 'absent' else 'replace' %}
<config xmlns="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0"
        xmlns:nc="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0">
  <native xmlns="http://cisco.com/ns/yang/Cisco-IOS-XE-native">
    <ip>
      <ssh>
        <server>
          <algorithm>
            <kex nc:operation="{{ op }}">
{% if state == 'present' %}
{% for algo in pq_ssh_kex %}
              <kex-options>{{ algo }}</kex-options>
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
            </kex>
            <hostkey nc:operation="{{ op }}">
{% if state == 'present' %}
{% for algo in pq_ssh_hostkey %}
              <hostkey-options>{{ algo }}</hostkey-options>
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
            </hostkey>
          </algorithm>
        </server>
      </ssh>
    </ip>
  </native>
</config>
